# EXPERIMENT 10 – INSTALL AND EXPLORE KARATE AND SPRING BOOT TESTING

## AIM
To install and explore Karate and Spring Boot testing frameworks.

## THEORY

*Karate:*
- Karate is an open-source testing framework.
- It supports API testing and automation.
- It uses simple feature files with readable syntax.
- It can test APIs without writing large amounts of Java code.

*Spring Boot:*
- Spring Boot is a Java framework used to develop applications.
- It simplifies application configuration.
- Spring Boot Test supports testing Spring applications.
- JUnit is commonly used to write and execute test cases.

## PART A – KARATE SETUP

### STEP 1: Check Java and Maven

powershell
java -version

mvn -version


### STEP 2: Create project

powershell
cd "C:\Users\Dell\Desktop"

mkdir karate-demo

cd karate-demo


### STEP 3: Create Maven pom.xml

xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
         https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>examples.demo</groupId>
    <artifactId>karate-demo</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.release>21</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
            <artifactId>karate-junit5</artifactId>
            <version>1.4.1</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.2.5</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>


### STEP 4: Create folders

powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path "src\test\java\examples\demo"

New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path "src\test\resources\examples\demo"


### STEP 5: Create sample.feature

Location:

src/test/resources/examples/demo/sample.feature

gherkin
Feature: Exploring Karate Testing

  Scenario: Test addition operation
    * def a = 10
    * def b = 20
    * def result = a + b
    * match result == 30

  Scenario: Test string value
    * def message = 'Hello Karate'
    * match message == 'Hello Karate'


### STEP 6: Create KarateTest.java

Location:

src/test/java/examples/demo/KarateTest.java

java
package examples.demo;

import com.intuit.karate.junit5.Karate;

class KarateTest {

    @Karate.Test
    Karate testSample() {
        return Karate.run("sample").relativeTo(getClass());
    }
}


### STEP 7: Run Karate test

powershell
mvn clean test


*Expected output:*

text
Tests run: 2
BUILD SUCCESS


The exact Maven output may contain additional details.

